My [previous post](https://zehao-zhou.hashnode.dev/soccer-1x2-markets-on-kalshi-and-polymarket-trade-counts-volume-and-timing) measured how much trading takes place in soccer 1X2 markets on Kalshi and Polymarket: counts, volume and timing, without looking at prices. This post looks at the prices. Using the same matched regular-time markets (home win, draw, away win), it asks how far apart the two venues' latest trades sit, which side of each market produced those trades, whether the difference is larger than the fees, and for how much of the match clock such a difference is standing.

The unit throughout is the **trade reference**. On each venue, fills within the same second on the same outcome and the same taker side are combined into one volume-weighted average price (VWAP) and treated as a single print. That print is carried forward until the venue prints again. Nothing here uses order books, quotes, depth, queue positions or my own fills: a gap between two references is something that happened on the tape, not a price that could have been hit. Every result below should be read with that constraint.

Charts follow the layout of the underlying notebook: one column per market group studied (defined in section 2) and, where the split matters, one row per outcome selection. Hover for exact values; each chart links to its data.

## Summary

::: {.summary-list}

1. **Crossing both venues does not pay after fees.** Taking liquidity on both legs (taker/taker) is positive on {{ k.screen.tt_pl_1 }} of Premier League in-play observations in the better direction, {{ k.screen.tt_wc_1 }} in the World Cup in play, and effectively never in the World Cup's last pregame hour ({{ k.screen.tt_wcpre_1 }}); the mean net result is about {{ k.screen.tt_pl_net }} c per contract pair. Two taker fees at a 50 c price cost {{ k.fee_round_trip }} c, while the mean absolute gap between the venues is {{ k.gap.pl.abs }} c in the Premier League and {{ k.gap.wc.abs }} c in the World Cup. Routes with one taker leg are positive on between {{ k.one_taker_min }} and {{ k.one_taker_max }} of observations depending on group, direction and outcome. Cross-venue *taking* on last-trade prices is not a strategy in these markets.

2. **The only route that is usually positive is resting on both venues.** With a passive leg on each venue (maker/maker), the two resting-side references overlap after fees on {{ k.screen.mm_pl_2 }}–{{ k.screen.mm_pl_1 }} of Premier League in-play observations and {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_2 }}–{{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_1 }} in the World Cup's last hour, with a mean net edge of {{ k.screen.mm_pl_net_2 }}–{{ k.screen.mm_pl_net_1 }} c in the Premier League and about {{ k.screen.mm_wc_net_1 }} c in the World Cup in play. What this route reads is the cross-venue bid–ask spread: one venue's bid-side print against the other venue's ask-side print. It has the shape of a cross-venue market-making position, not an arbitrage: both resting orders have to fill, and the tape does not say how often they would.

3. **Which venue should hold YES depends on the outcome.** For home and away wins, holding YES on Polymarket and NO on Kalshi is the better direction (maker/maker positive on {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_1 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_2 }} of Premier League observations). Polymarket prints lower than Kalshi overall (pooled gap {{ k.pair.pl_all_allall }} c; Kalshi's flow is buy-heavy and prints at the ask): for Premier League team wins, Polymarket's bid-side print averages {{ k.pair.pl_team_sellbuy }} c against Kalshi's ask side, while its ask-side print averages only {{ k.pair.pl_team_buysell }} c against Kalshi's bid side. The wider of those two cross-venue spreads is the one a resting Polymarket YES bid and a resting Kalshi NO bid capture. For draws the order flips: holding YES on Kalshi and NO on Polymarket is positive more often ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_2 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_1 }} in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_2 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_draw_1 }} in the Champions League, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_2 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_wc_draw_1 }} in the World Cup), because on draws Polymarket prints higher relative to Kalshi: its ask-side print averages {{ k.pair.pl_draw_buysell }} c against Kalshi's bid side in the Premier League and {{ k.pair.ucl_draw_buysell }} c in the Champions League, against {{ k.pair.pl_team_buysell }} c and {{ k.pair.ucl_team_buysell }} c for team wins.

4. **The draw is where the venues disagree most in both directions.** Draw references sit at least 1 c above Kalshi on {{ k.gap.pl_draw.higher }} of Premier League prints, against {{ k.gap.pl_team.higher }} for team wins. It is also where a one-taker route reaches its highest positive share: hitting Polymarket's bid while resting on Kalshi (taker/maker, K YES + PM NO) is positive on {{ k.screen.tm_pl_draw_2 }} of Premier League draw observations and {{ k.screen.tm_ucl_draw_2 }} in the Champions League, against {{ k.screen.tm_pl_team_2 }} for Premier League team wins. If a cross-venue strategy has a first candidate, it is the draw.

5. **Freshness sets the population, not the answer.** Club fixtures need a 60-second freshness cutoff to keep {{ k.pl60.both }} (Premier League) and {{ k.ucl60.both }} (Champions League) of the in-play clock comparable; the World Cup supports 5 seconds in play and 10 seconds in the last pregame hour. Yet the positive share of every route is nearly flat in the cutoff: maker/maker in the Premier League is {{ k.sweep.mm_pl_1 }} at F = 1 s and {{ k.sweep.mm_pl_300 }} at F = 300 s. What the cutoff changes is how much of the clock is in scope, and it is the sell-side leg that goes stale: the exact taker-side pairs that a route reads are fresh on only {{ k.pl_pair['sell/sell'].both }}–{{ k.pl_pair['buy/buy'].both }} of the Premier League in-play clock at 60 s, against {{ k.pl60.both }} pooled.

