What none of this measures: fill probability, queue position, adverse selection on the resting leg, and where the price goes after the observation. Those are the subject of the next study.

## Data and definitions

The sample contains **{{ fixtures }} fixtures across {{ competitions }} competition-season groups**, with scheduled kickoffs from **{{ first }} to {{ last }}**. Each fixture has a Polymarket and a Kalshi regular-time 1X2 market linked to the same API-Football fixture. The previous trade-count post used an earlier 1,942-fixture snapshot.

The time reference is **scheduled kickoff**, including for the in-play windows: elapsed wall-clock minutes, halftime included, not the match clock. "In play" means 0–105 minutes after scheduled kickoff; the 55-minute split used in one diagnostic is a clock split, not an observed halftime.

| Term | Meaning here |
| --- | --- |
| Print (trade reference) | Fills within one second on one venue, outcome and taker side, combined into a volume-weighted average price and carried forward until the next print. The pooled leg merges both taker sides. |
| Fresh pair | Both venues' latest prints are at most **F seconds** old; a pair's age is the age of its older print. F looks backward. |
| Observation | A print on either leg while the other leg is fresh. Per-print statistics weight each observation equally (the taker's view); per-second statistics weight it by how long the pair then stays fresh (the resting maker's view). |
| Forward horizon H | Whether the other venue prints strictly later and within **H seconds** of a trade. H looks forward; F and H are independent. |
| Signed gap | Polymarket price minus Kalshi price, in cents per $1 contract. Negative means the Polymarket reference is lower. |
| Route | A direction (which venue holds YES: `PM YES + K NO` or `K YES + PM NO`) and a role for each leg: taker crosses, maker rests. Route names give the **Polymarket role first**, then the Kalshi role. |
| Team win | Home and away priced separately, then pooled for reporting. It is not a combined contract. |

## 1. Where the trading is

{{ chart('3:0') }}

Trades per fixture is the number to keep in mind for everything that follows. The World Cup traded about {{ k.wc_vs_pl }}× the Premier League and {{ k.wc_vs_l1 }}× Ligue 1 per fixture, and Kalshi printed {{ k.k_over_pm_pl }}× as many trades as Polymarket per Premier League fixture and {{ k.k_over_pm_wc }}× per World Cup fixture. A single freshness cutoff therefore keeps a very different share of each group's clock, which is why the next section selects groups before comparing prices.

## 2. Is there a fresh pair to compare?

Availability is measured over the entire clock of every fixture, outcome and window, including the seconds in which nothing traded. A venue is fresh when its latest print is at most F seconds old.

### 2.1 Share of the clock with both venues fresh

{{ chart('5:0') }}

The World Cup reaches a high dual-fresh share within a few seconds. Club competitions need tens of seconds, and their two in-play halves differ by at most a few percentage points, so they are pooled below. The Women's Champions League 2025/26 group (four fixtures, no Kalshi trades) never has a fresh pair and is omitted from the availability charts.

### 2.2 Where the rest of the clock goes

At any instant, a market is in one of four states: both venues fresh, only Kalshi fresh, only Polymarket fresh, or neither. "Neither fresh" means neither venue traded in the preceding F seconds; it does not mean data are missing.

{{ chart('7:0') }}

At F = 60 s, the Premier League in-play clock splits into {{ k.pl60.both }} both fresh, {{ k.pl60.k }} only Kalshi fresh, {{ k.pl60.pm }} only Polymarket fresh and {{ k.pl60.neither }} neither; the Champions League into {{ k.ucl60.both }}, {{ k.ucl60.k }}, {{ k.ucl60.pm }} and {{ k.ucl60.neither }}. At F = 5 s, both venues are fresh for {{ k.wc5.both }} of the World Cup in-play clock but only {{ k.pl5.both }} of the Premier League's. The excluded clock is mostly one-sided: Kalshi has printed recently and Polymarket has not.

### 2.3 Groups entering the price study

A competition-season × window qualifies when both venues are fresh for at least 70% of its clock at F = 60 s (coverage rounded to a whole percent, which is how the Champions League qualifies at {{ k.ucl60.both }}). Each qualifying group then uses the smallest cutoff among 5, 10 and 60 s that reaches 70%. Four groups qualify:

{{ candidates }}

This is a scope rule for the fresh-pair comparisons, not a ranking of profitability. Groups that do not qualify simply have too little time with both venues recently active for a same-time price comparison; an asynchronous strategy in those groups is a different question.

### 2.4 Which leg goes stale

The four states above pool both taker sides of each venue. A route reads one side per venue: `PM YES + K NO` executed by two takers reads the Polymarket buy-side print and the Kalshi sell-side print. Here each leg is a single taker side, and a leg is fresh only when that side printed within F seconds.

{{ chart('11:0') }}

Per taker side, the picture is one-sided in a different way. In the Premier League at F = 60 s, the four pure pairs are fresh on only {{ k.pl_pair['sell/sell'].both }}–{{ k.pl_pair['buy/buy'].both }} of the in-play clock, against {{ k.pl60.both }} pooled, and the missing leg is each venue's sell side: `PM buy / K sell` spends {{ k.pl_pair['buy/sell'].pm }} of the clock with only Polymarket fresh, because Kalshi's sell-side print is stale, while `PM sell / K buy` spends {{ k.pl_pair['sell/buy'].k }} with only Kalshi fresh. `buy/buy` is the best-covered pair ({{ k.pl_pair['buy/buy'].both }}) and `sell/sell` the worst ({{ k.pl_pair['sell/sell'].both }}). In the World Cup's last pregame hour at F = 10 s the asymmetry is extreme: `PM sell / K buy` is {{ k.wcpre_sellbuy.k }} "only Kalshi fresh". Kalshi's buy-heavy flow, which section 4 sees from the price side, is the same fact seen from the activity side.

### 2.5 After a trade, how soon does the other venue trade?

This asks about trade seconds rather than the whole clock. For each source-venue trade second, does the destination venue print strictly later and within H seconds? Same-second prints do not count as a response, and a trigger is eligible only when its entire H-second future lies inside the window.

{{ chart('13:0') }}

The curves are hockey sticks. After a Kalshi trade second in the Premier League in play, Polymarket prints again within 1 / 5 / 10 / 60 s in {{ k.wait.pl_k[1] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[5] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[10] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[60] }} of eligible cases; the Champions League behaves the same ({{ k.wait.ucl_k[5] }} within 5 s). The reverse direction is faster everywhere ({{ k.wait.pl_pm[5] }} within 5 s in the Premier League), and the World Cup in play is at {{ k.wait.wc_k[5] }} (Kalshi → Polymarket) and {{ k.wait.wc_pm[5] }} (Polymarket → Kalshi) within 5 s.

When the destination venue was already stale at the trigger, the club rates fall sharply: at H = 60 s, {{ k.stale.pl_k }} for Kalshi → Polymarket and {{ k.stale.pl_pm }} for Polymarket → Kalshi in the Premier League, {{ k.stale.ucl_k }} and {{ k.stale.ucl_pm }} in the Champions League; in the World Cup the stale groups still exceed 98%. These are descriptive waiting probabilities. They do not show that the source trade causes or predicts the destination trade above its ordinary activity rate.

## 3. How far apart are the last trades?

An observation is a print on either venue while the other venue's latest print is at most F seconds old, at each group's own cutoff. The signed gap is Polymarket minus Kalshi, pooled over both taker sides; a gap of −2 c means the Polymarket reference is 2 c below Kalshi's.

{{ chart('16:0') }}

Per print weights each observation equally; per second weights it by how long the pair then stays fresh before the next print or expiry. The centre bucket holds gaps strictly between −1 and +1 c, the adjacent buckets hold the exact one-cent gaps both venues quote, and the end buckets are open at ±4 c.

For all outcomes pooled, the Premier League in-play sample has a **{{ k.gap.pl.abs }} c mean absolute gap** with {{ k.gap.pl.ge2 }} of fresh prints at least 2 c apart; the Champions League {{ k.gap.ucl.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl.ge2 }}; the World Cup in play {{ k.gap.wc.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.wc.ge2 }}; the World Cup's last hour {{ k.gap.wcpre.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.wcpre.ge2 }}. These use different cutoffs (60, 60, 5 and 10 s), so they mix activity and freshness effects.

The gap is not symmetric. In the Premier League, Polymarket prints at least 1 c **below** Kalshi on {{ k.gap.pl.lower }} of observations and at least 1 c **above** on {{ k.gap.pl.higher }}, a pooled mean of {{ k.gap.pl.mean }} c. The draw is more two-sided than team wins: Polymarket is at least 1 c above on {{ k.gap.pl_draw.higher }} of draw prints against {{ k.gap.pl_team.higher }} of team-win prints, and at least 1 c below on {{ k.gap.pl_draw.lower }} against {{ k.gap.pl_team.lower }}.

## 4. The trading side behind each gap

Two pooled last trades do not describe prices anyone could trade at. A taker buy and a taker sell hit different sides of the market, so the sign of a gap between a Polymarket buy-side print and a Kalshi sell-side print means something different from the sign between two buy-side prints. For the four pure taker-action pairs, the signed gap **is** the pre-fee gross edge of one route in each direction, `1 − p_YES − p_NO` with the legs that route reads. A taker leg is priced from the print on the side it would hit; a resting leg from the print on the side that would fill it:

| Pair (PM action / Kalshi action) | Reads | PM − K < 0 is the gross edge of | PM − K > 0 is the gross edge of |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| buy / sell | PM ask-side vs Kalshi bid-side print | taker/taker, `PM YES + K NO` (edge = −gap) | maker/maker, `K YES + PM NO` (edge = +gap): the cross-venue spread |
| sell / buy | PM bid-side vs Kalshi ask-side print | maker/maker, `PM YES + K NO` (edge = −gap): the cross-venue spread | taker/taker, `K YES + PM NO` (edge = +gap) |
| buy / buy | both ask-side | taker/maker, `PM YES + K NO` (−gap) | maker/taker, `K YES + PM NO` (+gap) |
| sell / sell | both bid-side | maker/taker, `PM YES + K NO` (−gap) | taker/maker, `K YES + PM NO` (+gap) |

{{ chart('18:0') }}

Each square shows the mean signed gap, the share of prints at least 2 c below and above zero, and the print count; blue means Polymarket printed lower, red higher.

The cross-side pairs are read by both two-taker and two-maker routes, with opposite signs. Their usual state is the ordinary cross-venue spread: for Premier League team wins, `sell/buy` averages {{ k.pair.pl_team_sellbuy }} c and `buy/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_team_buysell }} c, so a bid-side print on one venue sits 1–1.5 c below an ask-side print on the other. That is why the maker/maker route of section 5 is positive most of the time, and why the taker/taker route almost never is: it needs the same pair to cross zero, which happens on a few percent of prints. The asymmetry between the two numbers is the direction result: Polymarket's bid side sits further below Kalshi's ask side than Polymarket's ask side sits above Kalshi's bid side, so resting YES on Polymarket and NO on Kalshi reads the wider spread. For draws the wider spread is the other one (`sell/buy` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_sellbuy }} c, `buy/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_buysell }} c in the Premier League; {{ k.pair.ucl_draw_sellbuy }} c and {{ k.pair.ucl_draw_buysell }} c in the Champions League), which is why the better direction flips on draws. Pooled over both sides, Polymarket prints lower than Kalshi ({{ k.pair.pl_all_allall }} c in the Premier League, {{ k.pair.wc_all_allall }} c in the World Cup in play): Kalshi's flow is buy-heavy and prints at the ask, the same fact section 2.4 saw as Kalshi's stale sell side.

The same-side pairs are read by the one-maker routes. They are small (`buy/buy` {{ k.pair.pl_team_buybuy }} c and `sell/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_team_sellsell }} c for Premier League team wins, `sell/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_sellsell }} c for draws), which is why those routes rarely clear a taker fee.

Home and away are not separated because their pooled gaps agree to within 0.2 c in every group; the cross-venue mechanics are symmetric between them.

## 5. What survives fees

At every route-leg print with the other leg fresh, both legs are priced from their latest prints and the fee model below is subtracted. The reference net edge is `1 − PM cost − Kalshi cost − fees`.

Neither venue charges a flat fee. Both charge `rate × p × (1 − p)` per contract, largest at 50 c and zero at the extremes, where p is the price of the leg being bought (a NO leg pays on 1 − p; the curve is symmetric). Rates are the venues' published schedules, not historical billing, and no rebates are assumed:

| Leg | Rate | Rounding | At p = 50 c |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Polymarket taker | 5% | none | {{ k.fee50.pm_taker }} c per contract |
| Polymarket maker | 0 | — | 0 |
| Kalshi taker | 7% | rounded up to the cent on a 100-contract lot, plus the cent rounding of the lot's cash cost | {{ k.fee50.k_taker }} c per contract |
| Kalshi maker | 1.75% | same | {{ k.fee50.k_maker }} c per contract |

Fee per contract in cents at several prices (100-contract lots):

{{ fees }}

For a maker/maker position the Polymarket leg is priced from the Polymarket taker-sell print (the reference for a resting YES bid) and the Kalshi NO leg from one minus the Kalshi taker-buy print; the opposite direction reverses the sides.

{{ chart('21:0') }}

Bars show the share of fresh observations whose fee-adjusted reference edge is above zero. **The remainder, 100% minus the bar, is at or below zero:** for the three routes with a taker leg, that is the large majority of observations in every group. Taker/taker is positive on {{ k.screen.tt_pl_1 }} of Premier League in-play observations for `PM YES + K NO` and {{ k.screen.tt_pl_2 }} for `K YES + PM NO`, with a mean net result of {{ k.screen.tt_pl_net }} c; in the World Cup in play, {{ k.screen.tt_wc_1 }} and {{ k.screen.tt_wc_net }} c. After the fee curve, a two-taker trade on last-trade prices loses money on almost every observation.

Maker/maker is the only route positive on more than half of its observations, at {{ k.screen.mm_pl_1 }} (`PM YES + K NO`) and {{ k.screen.mm_pl_2 }} (`K YES + PM NO`) in the Premier League, {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_ucl_2 }} in the Champions League, {{ k.screen.mm_wc_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_wc_2 }} in the World Cup in play, and {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_1 }} and {{ k.screen.mm_wcpre_2 }} in its last pregame hour. Within that route, `PM YES + K NO` is the better direction for all outcomes pooled and for team wins ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_1 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_pl_team_2 }} in the Premier League), and `K YES + PM NO` is the better direction for draws in every group ({{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_2 }} versus {{ k.screen.mm_pl_draw_1 }} in the Premier League). The draw row also holds the highest one-taker positive share: taker/maker `K YES + PM NO` at {{ k.screen.tm_pl_draw_2 }} (Premier League) and {{ k.screen.tm_ucl_draw_2 }} (Champions League), against {{ k.screen.tm_pl_team_2 }} for Premier League team wins.

A maker/maker positive is easy to overread. It uses two passive-fill price references and assumes **both** resting legs fill at those prices. The screen does not measure joint fill probability, queue position, or the exposure while only one leg is filled. Sections 6 to 8 follow this route alone.

### Positive share across the freshness cutoff

The same screen at every cutoff from 1 to 300 s; the dotted line marks each group's selected F.

{{ chart('22:0') }}

The curves are almost flat. In the Premier League, maker/maker is positive on {{ k.sweep.mm_pl_1 }} of observations at F = 1 s, {{ k.sweep.mm_pl_10 }} at 10 s and {{ k.sweep.mm_pl_300 }} at 300 s; taker/taker moves from {{ k.sweep.tt_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.tt_pl_300 }}, taker/maker from {{ k.sweep.tm_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.tm_pl_300 }}, and maker/taker from {{ k.sweep.mt_pl_1 }} to {{ k.sweep.mt_pl_300 }}. The cutoff decides how much of the clock is compared, not what the comparison finds. It also means a looser cutoff admits stale references without visibly changing the rate, which is a reason to keep F tight when the result is used.

## 6. Resting on both venues: direction and freshness

Because F is swept here, each column is a competition × window and the selected cutoff plays no role. Each direction of maker/maker reads one pure taker-action pair of section 2.4: `PM YES + K NO` prices Polymarket's resting YES bid from the Polymarket sell print and Kalshi's resting NO bid from the Kalshi buy print (`PM sell / K buy`); `K YES + PM NO` reads `PM buy / K sell`; pooled sums both directions and uses the venue-level pair for the clock states.

{{ chart('24:0') }}

The six rows share the freshness axis:

- **Clock states** (rows 1–3): the four states of section 2.2 for the pair each direction reads, at F = 5, 10, …, 60 s. In the Premier League, `PM YES + K NO` has both legs fresh on {{ k.mm.pl_both_1_60 }} of the in-play clock at 60 s and `K YES + PM NO` on {{ k.mm.pl_both_2_60 }}; at 10 s the first direction is at {{ k.mm.pl_both_1_10 }}.
- **Positive rate** (row 4): the share of fresh observations with a positive fee-adjusted reference, the taker's view. It is flat in F, and the direction order is stable within each group: `PM YES + K NO` above `K YES + PM NO` in the club groups and in the World Cup in play, and by a wide margin in the World Cup's last hour ({{ k.mm.wcpre_pos_1_10 }} versus {{ k.mm.wcpre_pos_2_10 }} at 10 s).
- **Positive standing** (row 5): seconds in which a fresh, positive maker/maker reference stands, as a share of the outcome clock. This is the maker's view, the product of "both legs fresh" and "positive given fresh". It rises with F because the first factor does: in the Premier League, `PM YES + K NO` stands positive for {{ k.mm.pl_open_1_10 }} of the clock at 10 s and {{ k.mm.pl_open_1_60 }} at 60 s; in the World Cup in play, {{ k.mm.wc_open_1_5 }} already at 5 s and {{ k.mm.wc_open_1_60 }} at 60 s.
- **Mean net edge** (row 6): cents after fees per fresh print (solid) and per fresh second (dashed). Club groups sit around 1 c and the World Cup around 0.6 c in play; the per-second line is slightly higher, meaning positive references last a little longer than negative ones.

A positive edge here says that the two venues' resting-side prints overlapped after fees. It says nothing about whether either resting order would have filled, and a resting order that fills is the one on the wrong side of the next move more often than not. That adverse selection is unmeasured here.

## 7. Price level

The maker/maker positive share by the YES reference price on the venue holding YES, at each group's selected F, outcomes pooled. Each rate is positives divided by all observations **in that bucket**, not the share of positives contributed by the bucket.

{{ chart('28:0') }}

The fee curve peaks at 50 c, so mid-priced legs pay the most and screen worst. For `K YES + PM NO` in the Premier League, the positive share is {{ k.price.pl_2_low }} below 10 c, {{ k.price.pl_2_mid }} at 30–70 c and {{ k.price.pl_2_high }} at 70–90 c; the World Cup in play shows the same dip ({{ k.price.wc_2_mid }} and {{ k.price.wc_2_high }}). `PM YES + K NO` is steadier, at {{ k.price.pl_1_mid }} in the 30–70 c bucket and {{ k.price.pl_1_high }} at 70–90 c in the Premier League, with one exception: in the World Cup's last pregame hour its positive share drops to {{ k.price.wcpre_1_top }} for legs priced at 90 c or more.

## 8. How often, and for how long

The frequency of maker/maker positives per group at its selected F: positive observations per fixture-hour (fixtures × window length as the denominator), their distribution across fixtures, and the time-weighted share of the clock during which a fresh, positive reference is standing. The whole-clock share uses six clocks per fixture (three outcomes × two directions); the fresh-clock share counts only seconds with a fresh pair.

{{ frequency }}

Positives are frequent and spread out rather than concentrated: {{ k.freq.all_have }} fixture in every group shows at least one, and the ten busiest fixtures account for {{ k.freq.top10_min }}–{{ k.freq.top10_max }} of the total. A positive resting-side overlap stands for {{ k.freq.pl_clock }} of the Premier League in-play clock, {{ k.freq.ucl_clock }} in the Champions League, {{ k.freq.wc_clock }} in the World Cup in play and {{ k.freq.wcpre_clock }} in its last pregame hour. Several observations can belong to one continuing overlap, so these are not counts of independent trades.

## What is not measured

Everything above is a description of the trade tape. It establishes where the two venues print differently, on which side, by how much relative to fees, and for how long. It does not establish that any of it could be captured:

- There are no historical order books, so available size and queue position at the reference prices are unknown.
- A maker/maker positive assumes both passive legs fill. The tape does not give the joint fill probability, and the resting leg that fills is exposed to the next move.
- The price after each observation is not examined here. Whether a gap persists, closes toward one venue, or reverses is a separate measurement, as are markouts on the resting leg.
- Fees are the venues' current published schedules applied to historical trades; no rebates or tier discounts are assumed.
- No confidence intervals or fixture-clustered uncertainty are supplied. Comparisons are descriptive for this sample.

The next study takes the same observations forward in event time: how long a positive reference stands before a print removes it, what the other venue does next, and what a resting leg would have earned or lost against the subsequent prints.

## Data, methods and updates

This is a dated static snapshot. The page contains **{{ chart_count }} interactive charts**, each with its plotted aggregates downloadable as CSV and Plotly JSON, and a [snapshot manifest](data/manifest.json) with source hashes and the fixture date range. The [source notebook](source/02.1_cross_venue_gaps_by_competition.ipynb) is available without outputs; it requires the local research modules and curated inputs, so on its own it does not recreate the pipeline. The [publication notes](source/README.md) describe the build.

Method notes:

- Both venues' regular-time home/draw/away markets are linked to common API-Football fixtures. Market linkage is distinct from actual trading coverage.
- Availability is weighted by fixture × outcome × window seconds, including silence; intervals are split at window boundaries. Prints before the analysis window are unavailable, so initial freshness refers to the observed window.
- Gap observations occur when either leg prints and the other is fresh. Time weights use the duration until the next print, capped by the remaining freshness allowance.
- Group selection uses whole-percent coverage at F = 60 s and the smallest of 5, 10 and 60 s that reaches 70%.
- Trade waiting uses strictly later destination prints with forward-window boundary censoring (about 2% of triggers); rates are pooled over trade seconds, not averaged across fixtures.
- Fee scenarios are frozen analysis inputs. Kalshi fees are computed on 100-contract lots with the venue's rounding.
